{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794250777,"nonce":"8IRB/EUNXLzgR2nPaKHrtcHoOJ8=","title":"Overlay pass holds two full-window textures, one never drawn","message":"`render_overlay_msaa_flat` (and the layered variant) keep one cached pair, \"Overlay MSAA Texture\" and \"Overlay Resolve Texture\", at the viewport size. Every caller passes `sample_count = 1` (foreground, dynamic batch, dynamic image, overlay rendering). At one sample the pass renders straight into the resolve view and the MSAA texture is never a render target, yet it is allocated at full window size.\n\nMeasured on macOS (M-series, 1400x860 window at 2x, release), russl toy:\n- footprint 194 MB vs 105 MB for hello_blinc at the same size.\n- graphics (unmapped) 61 MB vs 28 MB. Texture log shows the live overlay pair at 2582x1720 Bgra8 (~18 MB each) as the difference.\n\nSecond problem: the cache has one slot keyed on `viewport_size`, and the overlay runs at several sizes in one frame (window, a 1790x1100 layer, a 290x27 layer). During the first second the slot thrashes: 84 texture allocations, about 1.4 GB allocated and dropped (31 full-window pairs). It stops once frames settle.\n\nFix candidates:\n1. Skip the MSAA texture when `sample_count == 1` (store it as `Option`). Saves one full-window texture, no behaviour change.\n2. Key the cache by size (small map) or size it to the largest seen and set the viewport, so alternating targets stop reallocating.\n3. Larger: at one sample with normal blend and opacity 1, draw into the target directly and drop the offscreen + composite.","files":null}]}